  "id": "DrugBank_DB00997 - MONDO_3792",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the pathogenesis of ovarian carcinosarcoma through a series of interconnected pathways involving chemotherapy-induced toxicity and tumor progression. The evidence suggests that Doxorubicin may exert a therapeutic effect on ovarian carcinosarcoma through mechanisms that involve the inhibition of tumor cell proliferation and the induction of apoptosis, as indicated by its use in treating Kaposi's sarcoma and malignant germ cell tumors of the ovary, both of which are associated with ovarian carcinosarcoma. The shared use of carboplatin in these contexts further suggests a potential role in the treatment of ovarian carcinosarcoma, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of a common downstream target, such as carboplatin, indicates a possible shared pathway in the treatment of ovarian carcinosarcoma, though the specific molecular mechanisms linking Doxorubicin to this disease remain under investigation. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Doxorubicin may have a role in the treatment of ovarian carcinosarcoma, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm this relationship.",
